<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Publicationethicseditorial  Policy - AJSMR</title><style>body{font-family:Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;margin:0;background:#f5f6f8;color:#222;line-height:1.65}.container{max-width:1050px;margin:35px auto;padding:0 20px}.card{background:#fff;border-radius:8px;padding:32px;box-shadow:0 2px 10px rgba(0,0,0,.08)}h1{margin-top:0;color:#222;font-size:30px}h2{font-size:21px;margin-top:28px;color:#333}li{margin:8px 0}.note{background:#f0f4f8;padding:14px 16px;border-left:4px solid #555;margin:20px 0}.small{font-size:13px;color:#666}</style></head><body><div class="container"><div class="card"><h1>Publication Ethics & Editorial Policy</h1><div class="note">AJSMR is committed to responsible scholarly publishing, transparent editorial processes, research integrity and protection of the scholarly record.</div><h2>Editorial Responsibility</h2><p>The Editor-in-Chief has overall responsibility for editorial decisions and for ensuring that stated policies are applied consistently. Decisions should be based on scholarly merit, scope, methodological quality and ethical compliance.</p><h2>Editorial Independence</h2><p>Editors should make decisions independently of commercial, financial or other inappropriate influence and manage conflicts of interest.</p><h2>Peer Review</h2><p>Primary research articles should undergo external peer review. The process stated on the website should correspond to the process actually used. Where the journal commits to two independent reviewers for research articles, the editorial system should record and support that practice.</p><h2>Fairness and Non-Discrimination</h2><p>Manuscripts should be assessed without discrimination based on personal characteristics or institutional affiliation, subject to legitimate journal requirements.</p><h2>Confidentiality</h2><p>Editors and reviewers must treat submitted manuscripts and associated information as confidential.</p><h2>Conflicts of Interest</h2><p>Authors, reviewers and editors should disclose relevant conflicts. An editor with a conflict should not make the final decision on the affected manuscript.</p><h2>Research Ethics</h2><p>Research involving humans, human data or animals should meet applicable ethical requirements, with relevant approval and consent information where required.</p><h2>Authorship</h2><p>Authorship should reflect substantive contributions. All authors should approve the submitted manuscript and accept responsibility for their contributions.</p><h2>Research Integrity</h2><p>AJSMR addresses plagiarism, duplicate publication, fabrication, falsification, inappropriate image manipulation and other research-integrity concerns under its research-integrity policy.</p><h2>Corrections, Expressions of Concern and Retractions</h2><p>When a published article contains a material error or integrity problem, AJSMR may publish a correction, expression of concern or retraction as appropriate.</p><h2>Complaints and Appeals</h2><p>Authors and other stakeholders may raise concerns or appeal editorial decisions through the journal's published complaints and appeals process.</p><h2>Artificial Intelligence</h2><p>AJSMR maintains a dedicated AI and Generative AI policy. AI tools must not be listed as authors, and authors remain responsible for accuracy and integrity. Confidential manuscript material should not be entered into external AI systems in ways that compromise confidentiality.</p><h2>Ethical Frameworks</h2><p>AJSMR may draw on recognized publication-ethics guidance, including COPE and relevant biomedical publishing recommendations, while applying procedures appropriate to the journal.</p><p class="small">AJSMR local testing version. Final wording should be confirmed against actual journal practices before live publication.</p></div></div></body></html>